Chapter: SmpHalt

 An idle core halts instead of busy-spinning, and is woken again to pick up
 work. This is the workload that exercises the wake path hardest: the boot
 program spawns one child at a time and waits on it, so between cycles three
 of four cores have nothing to run and halt on `hlt`. Each cycle the boot
 program itself blocks in `process-wait`, its core halts, the child runs on
 another core and exits, the exit wake loop marks the waiter READY, and a
 halted core must wake from its timer, scan, and resume it.

 A lost wake anywhere in that chain -- a waiter that commits to BLOCKED after
 the child's exit already scanned past it, a half-built spawn claimed before
 its slice is set, an idle core that halts and is never woken -- shows up as
 a hang: one of the waits never returns and the final line never prints. The
 spin is sized so the child outlives a scheduling tick, so the waiter really
 does block and its core really does halt rather than finding the child
 already done. Run it many times; a single hang is a failure.

Section: Body

  spin : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spin (n) (acc) = if n == 0 then acc else spin (n - 1) (acc + 1)

  child-body : Integer -> Integer
  child-body (x) = spin 50000000 0

  opening : [Console, Concurrent] Nothing = act
    a <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wa <- process-wait a
    b <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wb <- process-wait b
    c <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wc <- process-wait c
    d <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wd <- process-wait d
    e <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    we <- process-wait e
    f <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wf <- process-wait f
    g <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wg <- process-wait g
    h <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wh <- process-wait h
    print-line-uni "all waits returned"
  end
